I judged this from the diff and two quick git checks. I did not run the binary or call a real agent, because that needs a provider call.

• Gate and dispatcher in one commit: commit 40028335 contains both the new agent tool and the project-trust check, so there is no commit where a project agent can run without the gate.
• The gate:
  • It is a pure function and is checked twice: once when the tool is built, and again on every call against the live trust state. A /cd that drops trust therefore also blocks those agents.
  • Refusals reuse the existing --trust-project wording rather than a new flag name.
  • Agents whose tools line resolves to nothing are refused, naming the entries that failed.
• Registration: the tool is only added when at least one agent can run. It sits inside the --no-tools block and above the single disallowed-tools filter. Users with no agent files, or only untrusted project ones, see an unchanged tool list.
• Child agents: they are built by the same code sub_agent uses. In src/tools.rs the only change is making that builder visible to the new module. User deny rules, directory restrictions and --read-only-subagents therefore apply the same way.
• Tests: they cover the gate, the description (a sentinel string planted in each body is checked to be absent), the 8,000-byte cap with multi-byte text, the registration check, and the refusal paths when the tool is called.
• Docs: ARCHITECTURE.md and docs/src/usage/commands.md are updated. The docs went in a separate follow-up commit (c58eaead), not the gate commit; the task only required the gate and dispatcher to share a commit, so this is fine.
